findings.body:
The GTK plugin block was changed from `if !gtk.exists()` to `if gtk.exists()`. The gstreamer block a few lines below still uses `!exists()`. On a fresh tools directory the GTK script is never written, and linuxdeploy's `--plugin gtk` then fails. If a stale copy already exists, the embedded script is written over it, which is the opposite of what the other tools do.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="242-246"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The gstreamer block at line 249 uses `!gstreamer.exists()`, which is correct, so the two blocks now disagree.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="243-243"}
  if !gtk.exists() {

`write_and_make_executable` now calls `fs::set_permissions(path, ...)` before `fs::write(path, data)`. Every call site guards with `!x.exists()`, so the target usually does not exist yet. `set_permissions` on a missing path returns ENOENT, and the function returns `FileWriteError::Permissions` before any bytes are written. This affects AppRun, linuxdeploy, the gstreamer script, and the AppImage plugin. The original code wrote first and then chmod'ed, which is the required order.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="309-315"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The AppImage plugin's `Ok(data) => write_and_make_executable(...)?` arm propagates this error. The plugin was previously optional, so an error here now aborts the whole bundle instead of falling back to linuxdeploy's built-in plugin.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="312-313"}
  fs::write(path, data).map_err(FileWriteError::Write)?;
  fs::set_permissions(path, fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o770)).map_err(FileWriteError::Permissions)?;

Callers of `write_and_make_executable` use `?` inside `prepare_tools`, which returns `crate::Result`. Before this PR, the function returned `std::io::Error`, and `crate::Error` has `#[from] io::Error` on `IoError`, so the conversion worked. `FileWriteError` is a new type with no `From` impl into `crate::Error`, and `crate::Error` has no blanket conversion from `std::error::Error` either. The `?` at the AppRun, linuxdeploy, and gstreamer call sites will therefore fail with a `From<FileWriteError>` is not implemented error. The `Ok(data) => ...?` arm at line 259 has the same problem.

The simplest fix is to keep returning `std::io::Error` and drop the custom enum, or to add an explicit `From<FileWriteError>` for `crate::Error`. The enum adds no value unless callers match on the variant, and none do.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="284-309"}

The code still computes `linuxdeploy_arch` (mapping `i686` to `i383`) and uses it for the local filename. The download URL, however, was changed from `linuxdeploy-{linuxdeploy_arch}.AppImage` to `linuxdeploy-{arch}.AppImage`. This PR does not touch that mapping's purpose, so for i686 the download now requests `linuxdeploy-i686.AppImage`. The release asset may only exist under the old `i383` name, which would turn into a 404 on 32-bit builds.

The PR diff does not explain why this changed. If the asset is really named `i686`, the local filename and URL should be consistent. If it is named `i383`, the URL should keep `linuxdeploy_arch`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="235-240"}

Embedding the two plugin scripts with `include_bytes!` removes a runtime fetch of code from `raw.githubusercontent.com`, and the bundler now runs code that was reviewed in-repo and shipped with the crate. This fixes the 429 problem and also removes an unpinned `master` branch fetch, which is a supply-chain improvement. The `.changes` entry is also correctly scoped as a patch.

::callout{type="security"}
The scripts are still executed as linuxdeploy plugins, so they keep the bundler's privileges. Pinning them to the crate version is the right trust boundary.
